# Shell scripts must keep LF. core.autocrlf=true is the norm on Windows, and
# without this rule a fresh clone checks scripts/*.sh out with CRLF - bash then
# rejects the shebang, because the interpreter path ends in a stray \r. The
# blobs are already stored with LF; this is purely about what checkout writes.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
# Source, data and docs are LF everywhere too. With core.autocrlf=true they
# would otherwise check out as CRLF, and agent tools that write LF-only
# multi-line replacements then fail to match the CRLF text on disk.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
# cmd.exe reads batch files most reliably with CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
